1
00:00:01,070 --> 00:00:05,230
Okay, last section of this chapter, gestational pathology.

2
00:00:05,810 --> 00:00:09,290
The first disorder that we want to talk about is ectopic pregnancy.

3
00:00:09,290 --> 00:00:15,030
So the idea here is that we get implantation of a fertilized ovum at a site other than the uterine wall.

4
00:00:15,450 --> 00:00:20,930
The most common site is the lumen of the fallopian tube, and the key risk factor is scarring.

5
00:00:21,170 --> 00:00:25,750
So let's remind ourselves, when might we see scarring in the lumen of the fallopian tube?

6
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:34,510
Well, one example would be pelvic inflammatory disease, where the patients get inflammation of the upper portion of the female genital tract,

7
00:00:34,950 --> 00:00:38,810
and so that could result in scarring, which would increase the risk of ectopic pregnancy.

8
00:00:39,510 --> 00:00:42,030
And another example would be endometriosis.

9
00:00:42,150 --> 00:00:47,490
Remember that if endometriosis involves a tube, then there can be damage to the tube with scarring,

10
00:00:47,590 --> 00:00:53,410
which could then result in an ectopic pregnancy as well, in particular, an ectopic tubal pregnancy.

11
00:00:53,410 --> 00:00:55,850
Here's what an ectopic pregnancy would look like.

12
00:00:55,930 --> 00:00:57,110
This is the fallopian tube.

13
00:00:57,530 --> 00:00:59,750
Clearly, this region of the fallopian tube is expanded,

14
00:01:00,130 --> 00:01:04,310
and you can see this area of hemorrhage at this point where the tube is actually ruptured.

15
00:01:04,890 --> 00:01:08,530
The classic presentation is that the patient comes in with lower quadrant abdominal pain.

16
00:01:08,970 --> 00:01:13,350
Now, when a patient comes in with lower quadrant abdominal pain, there's a lot of things that you can think about.

17
00:01:13,770 --> 00:01:18,790
But one of the sort of general principles is anytime you have someone with lower quadrant abdominal pain

18
00:01:18,790 --> 00:01:23,970
and you're worried about anything severe, you always want to think about doing a pregnancy test,

19
00:01:24,030 --> 00:01:27,850
especially in a woman that's within that range of age in which she could be pregnant.

20
00:01:28,270 --> 00:01:33,330
So pregnancy test is a very important initial test in a patient that's got abdominal pain,

21
00:01:33,690 --> 00:01:35,890
presenting, for example, as an acute abdomen.

22
00:01:36,530 --> 00:01:40,170
So that's a classic presentation, and it's potentially a surgical emergency

23
00:01:40,170 --> 00:01:42,030
because obviously the tube can rupture.

24
00:01:42,870 --> 00:01:46,790
Another possible disorder of gestation is called a spontaneous abortion.

25
00:01:46,790 --> 00:01:50,950
This is where there's a miscarriage of the fetus, usually 20 weeks before gestation,

26
00:01:51,770 --> 00:01:57,810
so at 20 weeks or before, and so that would be a spontaneous abortion.

27
00:01:59,170 --> 00:02:05,390
Now, what we should recognize is that this is relatively common, so that's one point.

28
00:02:05,810 --> 00:02:10,410
And another point is that it is recognizable in up to one-fourth of pregnancies,

29
00:02:10,590 --> 00:02:12,470
so that's how common it actually is.

30
00:02:12,870 --> 00:02:13,850
How will the patients present?

31
00:02:13,850 --> 00:02:17,990
They have vaginal bleeding with cramp-like pain, and they pass fetal tissue.

32
00:02:18,170 --> 00:02:22,090
So that's the classic presentation of a spontaneous abortion.

33
00:02:22,610 --> 00:02:27,430
What's important to remember is that this is relatively common, but it's due to chromosomal anomalies.

34
00:02:27,630 --> 00:02:30,650
And, you know, it's not due to exercise or stress, etc.

35
00:02:30,830 --> 00:02:33,650
It's most often thought to be due to chromosomal anomalies.

36
00:02:34,130 --> 00:02:38,590
There are some other important causes to be aware of, but the classic cause is chromosomal anomalies.

37
00:02:38,590 --> 00:02:41,810
One important cause to be aware of would be hypercoagulable state.

38
00:02:42,230 --> 00:02:50,370
Can you remind me which patient that we've already talked about in a prior chapter could develop recurrent pregnancy loss

39
00:02:50,370 --> 00:02:53,850
as part of the complication of their hypercoagulable state?

40
00:02:54,630 --> 00:02:59,690
Remember, patients with lupus who have lupus anticoagulant get a hypercoagulable state,

41
00:02:59,690 --> 00:03:04,550
and one of the ways by which that can manifest itself is with recurrent pregnancy loss.

42
00:03:05,010 --> 00:03:07,910
Congenital infection can result in a spontaneous abortion,

43
00:03:08,330 --> 00:03:11,710
and exposure to teratogens can result in a spontaneous abortion.

44
00:03:12,210 --> 00:03:15,730
Now, when you think about teratogens, it's a little more, it's a little complex.

45
00:03:15,970 --> 00:03:18,050
It depends on when they're actually exposed.

46
00:03:18,050 --> 00:03:23,970
So, for example, when you have exposure to a teratogen, if it's within the first two weeks

47
00:03:23,970 --> 00:03:28,890
and the dose is significant and it's a significant teratogen, that can result in spontaneous abortion.

48
00:03:29,670 --> 00:03:35,230
Classically, if it occurs between weeks three to weeks eight, then you think about a risk of organ malformation.

49
00:03:35,730 --> 00:03:38,750
And if it occurs after that, any time from months three to months nine,

50
00:03:39,130 --> 00:03:41,750
then you think about a risk of organ hypoplasia.

51
00:03:42,190 --> 00:03:44,290
And so these are some of the things that can occur with teratogens.

52
00:03:44,290 --> 00:03:50,650
Now, in your book, I've put in a list of teratogens, a table, with some very classic high-yield changes that can occur,

53
00:03:51,030 --> 00:03:52,670
and so I'll ask you to review that there.

54
00:03:52,770 --> 00:03:55,110
I really don't need to read to you.

55
00:03:55,530 --> 00:03:58,770
Most of the explanation is just in the principles, and I'm giving you that.

56
00:03:59,770 --> 00:04:04,750
Placenta previa is an implantation of the placenta in the lower uterine segment.

57
00:04:04,890 --> 00:04:05,990
And it's very simple.

58
00:04:06,110 --> 00:04:07,670
Here's basically the way you would think about it.

59
00:04:07,970 --> 00:04:10,670
This is the cervix, and then this is the uterine wall.

60
00:04:10,970 --> 00:04:13,390
And normally, the placenta would implant somewhere up here.

61
00:04:13,390 --> 00:04:20,930
If the placenta implants here in the lower uterine segment, what's going to happen is that you're going to get a preview of the placenta, right?

62
00:04:20,990 --> 00:04:23,630
Either you're going to be able to see it, or you're going to be able to feel it.

63
00:04:23,870 --> 00:04:27,090
So it's as if you get a preview of the placenta, and that's placenta previa.

64
00:04:27,630 --> 00:04:30,270
It's when the placenta overlies the cervical os.

65
00:04:30,630 --> 00:04:36,210
This presents as third trimester bleeding, and it often requires delivery of the fetus by C-section.

66
00:04:36,210 --> 00:04:42,950
And that's because, imagine if there's baby, and baby's trying to get through here, as baby tries to get through here, that's going to compress the placenta.

67
00:04:43,310 --> 00:04:47,610
And if you compress the placenta, you're basically compressing the blood source of the baby.

68
00:04:47,950 --> 00:04:49,570
And so that could result in fetal distress.

69
00:04:50,190 --> 00:05:00,030
And so placenta previa, if it really exists and it continues throughout the very late portion of the pregnancy, it would be treated with a, or it would require a C-section.

70
00:05:00,030 --> 00:05:04,970
Another complication is called placental abruption, and what's placental abruption?

71
00:05:05,170 --> 00:05:11,670
That would be where you have a placenta, like this, and the placenta abruptly begins to separate from the uterine wall.

72
00:05:12,170 --> 00:05:15,270
So that's placenta abruption, and that's what I've stated here.

73
00:05:15,590 --> 00:05:19,310
Separation of the placenta from the decidua prior to delivery.

74
00:05:19,770 --> 00:05:24,810
This is a common cause of stillbirth, and it presents with third trimester bleeding and fetal insufficiency.

75
00:05:24,810 --> 00:05:40,750
And that kind of makes sense, because if you go back here, and you've got this uterus, and you've got this placenta up here, if you begin to separate the placenta from the wall, well, then you've cut off the source of, you know, the baby's source of blood, or the baby's source of oxygen, I should say.

76
00:05:41,050 --> 00:05:44,390
And so what's going to happen then is that that's going to result in fetal distress.

77
00:05:44,850 --> 00:05:47,490
So there'll be fetal insufficiency and a risk of stillbirth.

78
00:05:47,850 --> 00:05:53,350
And at the same time, as this placenta begins to separate, you're going to get bleeding between the placenta and the area of separation.

79
00:05:53,350 --> 00:05:56,010
And so that's going to result in third trimester bleeding.

80
00:05:56,430 --> 00:06:07,070
Classically, when you remove the placenta in these patients with placental abruption, you see that there is blood on the surface of the placenta that was up against the decidua, the maternal surface.

81
00:06:07,170 --> 00:06:12,970
That's called the maternal surface, and there'll be a very bloody maternal surface with clots along its surface.

82
00:06:14,150 --> 00:06:20,190
Placenta accreta is when you have improper implantation of the placenta directly into the myometrium.

83
00:06:20,190 --> 00:06:31,210
Now, let's review this and remind ourselves that if this is the uterine wall, then what you've got here is decidua, which is really endometrium that has been put under the effect of progesterone.

84
00:06:31,690 --> 00:06:34,570
And then after that, you've got myometrium, right?

85
00:06:34,570 --> 00:06:41,310
And so normally, when the placenta comes in, the placenta will come in here, and it will attach to the decidua.

86
00:06:41,970 --> 00:06:44,210
That's the normal implantation of the placenta.

87
00:06:44,570 --> 00:06:53,830
However, if there is no decidua or there's minimal decidua, the placenta will attach to the myometrium, and when it attaches to the myometrium, that's called placenta accreta.

88
00:06:53,970 --> 00:06:54,910
It becomes stuck.

89
00:06:55,430 --> 00:06:56,590
Now, how is this going to present?

90
00:06:56,770 --> 00:06:59,830
This is going to present with difficulty in delivering the placenta.

91
00:06:59,830 --> 00:07:03,870
Remember that after the baby is delivered, the next thing we do is we deliver the placenta.

92
00:07:04,350 --> 00:07:07,310
However, the placenta is stuck, so we won't be able to deliver it very well.

93
00:07:07,590 --> 00:07:09,470
So that's the difficult delivery of the placenta.

94
00:07:09,930 --> 00:07:15,810
And they get postpartum bleeding, because as that placenta is sort of partially off and partially on, there's going to be tremendous bleeding.

95
00:07:16,310 --> 00:07:20,150
And this often results in hysterectomy, because the placenta can't be taken off,

96
00:07:20,490 --> 00:07:26,550
and so the entire uterus has to be removed because of the massive amounts of blood loss that are occurring during that delivery.

97
00:07:26,550 --> 00:07:34,530
Pre-eclampsia is pregnancy-induced hypertension and proteinuria, and the patients also have edema because they lose protein in their urine.

98
00:07:34,970 --> 00:07:41,270
This classically arises in the third trimester, and it's a problem of the maternal-fetal-vascular interface.

99
00:07:41,810 --> 00:07:45,090
Basically, when you have pre-eclampsia, it's due to the placenta.

100
00:07:45,230 --> 00:07:48,750
Something is happening with the placenta that results in this complication.

101
00:07:49,490 --> 00:07:50,610
Now, a few important points.

102
00:07:50,670 --> 00:07:53,110
First of all, this is seen in about 5% of pregnancies.

103
00:07:53,110 --> 00:07:57,650
The patients can have hypertension, and the hypertension can be really severe.

104
00:07:57,830 --> 00:08:05,650
It can be almost to the level of malignant hypertension, where the patients actually get headaches and visual abnormalities, et cetera.

105
00:08:06,350 --> 00:08:11,450
Now, one thing to bear in mind is that when you have a patient with pre-eclampsia, you're going to have very high blood pressure,

106
00:08:11,550 --> 00:08:16,590
and that very high blood pressure is going to result in a classic finding, which is called fibrinoid necrosis.

107
00:08:16,590 --> 00:08:20,810
And that fibrinoid necrosis could be seen in the vessels of the placenta.

108
00:08:21,430 --> 00:08:23,230
So this is a very important association.

109
00:08:23,450 --> 00:08:28,630
That is that patients with pre-eclampsia may have fibrinoid necrosis in the vessels of the placenta.

110
00:08:29,310 --> 00:08:33,630
Of course, this is due to an abnormality of the placenta at the fetal-maternal interface,

111
00:08:33,630 --> 00:08:36,770
and so if you remove the placenta, this will actually resolve.

112
00:08:37,150 --> 00:08:38,090
That's pre-eclampsia.

113
00:08:38,550 --> 00:08:42,810
Now, if a patient has pre-eclampsia, and then they subsequently on top of that develop seizures,

114
00:08:43,030 --> 00:08:46,670
that's called eclampsia, and that mandates delivery of the placenta.

115
00:08:46,790 --> 00:08:53,050
So when a patient's got eclampsia, then the baby has to be delivered, the placenta has to be removed because of that complication, too.

116
00:08:53,310 --> 00:08:55,590
And, of course, that would resolve the eclampsia.

117
00:08:55,590 --> 00:09:00,910
Another complication of pre-eclampsia or a progression of pre-eclampsia would be called HELP.

118
00:09:01,550 --> 00:09:07,690
H stands for hemolysis, EL stands for elevated liver enzymes, and LP stands for low platelets.

119
00:09:07,930 --> 00:09:17,670
This is an example of a thrombotic microangiopathy in which the patients get thrombi, in particular platelet thrombi,

120
00:09:17,870 --> 00:09:19,690
within the small blood vessels of the liver.

121
00:09:19,690 --> 00:09:24,690
So the idea is the following, and you're familiar with this now by the time we've gotten to this discussion,

122
00:09:24,690 --> 00:09:27,550
and that is that you've got the liver here.

123
00:09:27,670 --> 00:09:33,990
Now, this is a blood vessel going through the liver, and we get a little bit of a platelet thrombus here,

124
00:09:34,210 --> 00:09:36,590
and that platelet thrombus is in a small vessel.

125
00:09:36,870 --> 00:09:41,730
When the red blood cell tries to go across it, it gets sheared, and a schistocyte is going to be produced.

126
00:09:42,210 --> 00:09:47,270
So these patients are going to have hemolysis because they're shearing their red blood cells.

127
00:09:47,270 --> 00:09:54,610
They're going to have elevated liver enzymes because this is going to result in infarction of some of the liver tissue,

128
00:09:54,690 --> 00:09:57,870
because there's going to be a lack of good blood flow going through the liver,

129
00:09:57,870 --> 00:10:03,690
and they're going to have low platelets because these platelets are being used up to form these little thrombi,

130
00:10:03,910 --> 00:10:05,050
these platelet thrombi.

131
00:10:05,430 --> 00:10:08,170
And so that's the classic findings of HELP.

132
00:10:08,590 --> 00:10:10,710
Now, what do we say here in your text?

133
00:10:10,770 --> 00:10:14,570
We say that it's preeclampsia with thrombotic microangiopathy involving the liver.

134
00:10:14,970 --> 00:10:20,090
There's hemolysis, elevated liver enzyme, and low platelets, which basically the name tells you.

135
00:10:20,530 --> 00:10:23,310
Next disorder is called sudden infant death syndrome.

136
00:10:23,730 --> 00:10:26,130
This is where a child dies suddenly.

137
00:10:26,130 --> 00:10:31,070
It's a healthy infant, dies for some unknown cause, or really without any direct cause.

138
00:10:31,590 --> 00:10:34,630
Classically, it must occur between one month to one year of age.

139
00:10:34,950 --> 00:10:38,350
So if a child is greater than a year, we don't call that sudden infant death syndrome.

140
00:10:38,550 --> 00:10:41,390
If they're less than a month, we don't call that sudden infant death syndrome.

141
00:10:41,930 --> 00:10:46,590
Patients usually die while they're sleeping, and the risk factors include sleeping on the stomach.

142
00:10:46,590 --> 00:10:52,790
Now, also, smoking in the household increases the risk, and that is something that examiners like to go after,

143
00:10:52,910 --> 00:10:58,290
because, again, we always want to be able to alert our patients to the risks of smoking.

144
00:10:58,710 --> 00:11:01,570
So this is something to be aware of, sudden infant death syndrome.

145
00:11:02,630 --> 00:11:06,810
The next and last discussion in this section deals with hydatidiform mull.

146
00:11:07,590 --> 00:11:08,670
What is hydatidiform mull?

147
00:11:08,670 --> 00:11:14,150
Well, basically, the idea here is that you have a conception, but it's an abnormal conception,

148
00:11:14,350 --> 00:11:19,830
and instead of growing a baby, you grow abnormal placental tissue.

149
00:11:20,090 --> 00:11:21,530
That's the underlying idea.

150
00:11:21,970 --> 00:11:23,110
You get these villi.

151
00:11:23,170 --> 00:11:26,230
Remember that the villi are the functional unit of the placenta.

152
00:11:26,650 --> 00:11:29,750
So you get these villi, and the villi are very edematous,

153
00:11:30,170 --> 00:11:34,670
and those edematous villi have excessive proliferation of the cells around them,

154
00:11:34,670 --> 00:11:39,710
and remember that the cells that surround the villi are the trophoblast, and that's called the hydatidiform mull.

155
00:11:39,990 --> 00:11:45,190
So it's an abnormal conception characterized by swollen and edematous villi

156
00:11:45,190 --> 00:11:48,270
with proliferation of trophoblasts around those villi.

157
00:11:48,710 --> 00:11:53,890
Now, when a woman gets a hydatidiform mull, she doesn't know that she's got that mull growing within her.

158
00:11:54,250 --> 00:11:58,930
Her uterus is going to expand, and she's going to think that she's got a normal pregnancy.

159
00:11:58,930 --> 00:12:04,670
A couple unique things that will be seen in this patient is that the uterus will be bigger than normal.

160
00:12:05,050 --> 00:12:11,490
So, for example, her uterus would be larger than what you would expect to be for her dates of gestation.

161
00:12:11,870 --> 00:12:16,570
And number two, her beta-HCG will be higher than you would expect for those dates of gestation.

162
00:12:16,930 --> 00:12:21,230
You can look at the beta-HCG along the chart or timeline of gestation,

163
00:12:21,310 --> 00:12:25,570
and as you progress through the gestation, the beta-HCG goes up, especially in the first trimester.

164
00:12:25,570 --> 00:12:30,750
And so in this particular case, she's going to seem like she's got a normal pregnancy.

165
00:12:30,870 --> 00:12:33,230
The uterus will get bigger and the beta-HCG will be up.

166
00:12:33,450 --> 00:12:38,870
However, the uterus will be bigger than normal, and the beta-HCG would be higher than normal.

167
00:12:39,510 --> 00:12:43,570
Now, eventually what will happen, or classically what would happen if there was no prenatal care,

168
00:12:43,930 --> 00:12:47,110
that the woman would begin to pass these little grape-like masses.

169
00:12:47,650 --> 00:12:48,990
See how these kind of look like grapes?

170
00:12:48,990 --> 00:12:56,090
So these are the grape-like masses that a woman with a molar pregnancy would actually pass through the vaginal canal.

171
00:12:56,430 --> 00:12:57,990
And that's the classic presentation.

172
00:12:58,410 --> 00:13:02,730
Usually it occurs in the early second trimester, and that would be seen if there's no prenatal care.

173
00:13:03,130 --> 00:13:04,430
Now, what are those grape-like masses?

174
00:13:04,630 --> 00:13:09,550
Remember that the normal structure of the placenta, the functional unit, is called the villus.

175
00:13:09,610 --> 00:13:13,510
So this would be the villus, and the villus is surrounded by trophoblastic cells,

176
00:13:13,590 --> 00:13:15,970
like the cytotrophoblast and the syncytial trophoblast.

177
00:13:15,970 --> 00:13:22,530
Well, in this particular disorder, you've got these abnormal villi, and the villi are very edematous,

178
00:13:22,970 --> 00:13:27,510
and often surrounded by trophoblasts, sometimes excessive trophoblasts.

179
00:13:27,650 --> 00:13:32,570
So it's these large edematous villi that actually pass through as grape-like masses,

180
00:13:32,790 --> 00:13:35,430
and that's why the patient's passing these grape-like masses.

181
00:13:36,490 --> 00:13:39,310
Now, more commonly in the West, where there might be prenatal care,

182
00:13:39,750 --> 00:13:43,130
the patients wouldn't be diagnosed by the passing of grape-like masses,

183
00:13:43,130 --> 00:13:46,630
but instead they would undergo routine ultrasound in the early first trimester.

184
00:13:47,050 --> 00:13:50,930
And what would be found is that there is no baby, so the heart sounds would be absent.

185
00:13:51,410 --> 00:13:54,790
And instead, you see all of these grape-like masses on ultrasound,

186
00:13:54,990 --> 00:13:57,650
and those grape-like masses, because they're full of fluid,

187
00:13:57,970 --> 00:14:02,990
they produce this white and black sort of discrepancy between where the masses are

188
00:14:02,990 --> 00:14:06,850
and what's in between the masses, and that's called a snowstorm appearance.

189
00:14:06,850 --> 00:14:11,730
So that's a classic finding an ultrasound when there is prenatal care.

190
00:14:12,070 --> 00:14:15,190
So passing of grape-like masses if there is no prenatal care,

191
00:14:15,530 --> 00:14:20,590
and a snowstorm appearance with a lack of fetal heart sounds if there is prenatal care.

192
00:14:20,950 --> 00:14:24,730
Now, once you've made a decision that the patient has a molar pregnancy,

193
00:14:25,330 --> 00:14:29,210
then the next thing you have to do is you have to classify that molar pregnancy.

194
00:14:29,690 --> 00:14:33,070
And molar pregnancies can be classified as complete or partial.

195
00:14:33,670 --> 00:14:36,550
Now, the classification of complete or partial is based on a few things.

196
00:14:36,550 --> 00:14:39,650
So you look at the genetics, you look at the fetal tissue,

197
00:14:40,190 --> 00:14:43,430
and you ask the question, is there any fetal tissue being made or not?

198
00:14:43,570 --> 00:14:45,170
Sometimes there is, sometimes there isn't.

199
00:14:45,950 --> 00:14:47,590
You look at how swollen the villi are.

200
00:14:48,010 --> 00:14:49,690
Sometimes all of the villi are swollen.

201
00:14:50,050 --> 00:14:51,770
Sometimes only some of the villi are swollen.

202
00:14:52,150 --> 00:14:53,890
You look at the trophoblastic proliferation.

203
00:14:54,070 --> 00:14:56,490
In these particular disorders, you've got the swollen villi,

204
00:14:56,850 --> 00:15:01,190
and sometimes there will be excess trophoblasts all along the villus,

205
00:15:01,270 --> 00:15:02,350
like from top to bottom,

206
00:15:02,770 --> 00:15:06,070
and sometimes there will be excess trophoblastic proliferation

207
00:15:06,070 --> 00:15:08,330
only on one end of the villus.

208
00:15:08,750 --> 00:15:10,450
And so that's a question that you have to ask.

209
00:15:10,590 --> 00:15:13,850
How extensive is the trophoblastic proliferation?

210
00:15:14,350 --> 00:15:17,250
And all of these features are useful

211
00:15:17,250 --> 00:15:21,710
for trying to subclassify a mole as partial or complete.

212
00:15:21,710 --> 00:15:25,370
Now, you could sit here and try to memorize this chart,

213
00:15:25,450 --> 00:15:27,050
and you probably could do it,

214
00:15:27,050 --> 00:15:30,470
but I think there's a little bit of an easier way to remember this.

215
00:15:30,650 --> 00:15:32,770
And for me, I always like to have a principle

216
00:15:32,770 --> 00:15:35,390
and then try to be able to derive as many of the points

217
00:15:35,390 --> 00:15:36,830
from that principle as possible.

218
00:15:37,230 --> 00:15:38,710
So I'll tell you how I think about this,

219
00:15:38,730 --> 00:15:40,270
and hopefully it'll be useful to you.

220
00:15:40,810 --> 00:15:43,870
Now, the way I think about it is to understand why it's occurring.

221
00:15:44,350 --> 00:15:46,910
So let's have a little discussion as to why this occurs.

222
00:15:46,910 --> 00:15:50,410
As with most things in life, it's usually dad's fault, right?

223
00:15:50,690 --> 00:15:54,510
So this actually is a disorder that is basically dad's fault.

224
00:15:55,010 --> 00:15:58,850
Now, what happens is one possibility is that there is an egg,

225
00:15:59,370 --> 00:16:01,810
an ovum, and the ovum is empty.

226
00:16:02,590 --> 00:16:05,270
And two sperm come into that ovum.

227
00:16:05,970 --> 00:16:08,530
So if two sperm come into that ovum,

228
00:16:08,810 --> 00:16:10,910
that can result in a molar pregnancy.

229
00:16:11,550 --> 00:16:13,830
Now, when two sperm come into an empty ovum,

230
00:16:13,830 --> 00:16:15,990
all of the genetic material is dad, correct?

231
00:16:16,530 --> 00:16:17,890
So that is a complete mole.

232
00:16:18,050 --> 00:16:20,810
It is completely from dad.

233
00:16:21,390 --> 00:16:23,390
All the genetic material is completely from dad.

234
00:16:23,630 --> 00:16:25,010
So let's just ask a simple question.

235
00:16:25,110 --> 00:16:26,790
If I only have two sperm in here,

236
00:16:26,870 --> 00:16:28,350
then how many chromosomes would I have?

237
00:16:28,770 --> 00:16:30,270
I would have 46 chromosomes.

238
00:16:30,730 --> 00:16:33,530
So in a complete mole, you have only 46 chromosomes.

239
00:16:34,150 --> 00:16:35,730
Now, another thing that we were going to look at

240
00:16:35,730 --> 00:16:37,790
was whether fetal tissue was present or absent.

241
00:16:38,150 --> 00:16:40,790
Well, I'll tell you that when you have an empty egg with two sperm,

242
00:16:40,790 --> 00:16:41,850
i.e. a complete mole,

243
00:16:42,150 --> 00:16:43,550
you don't get any fetal tissue.

244
00:16:44,130 --> 00:16:44,850
So what could I,

245
00:16:44,910 --> 00:16:46,110
the other way I could think about this

246
00:16:46,110 --> 00:16:48,250
is it's completely a mole.

247
00:16:48,630 --> 00:16:49,770
It's completely molar.

248
00:16:49,990 --> 00:16:52,130
There's no other baby tissue there.

249
00:16:52,190 --> 00:16:53,070
It's just all mole.

250
00:16:53,390 --> 00:16:54,730
So that's a complete mole.

251
00:16:54,810 --> 00:16:55,630
It's a complete mole.

252
00:16:55,770 --> 00:16:56,670
There's no baby tissue.

253
00:16:57,250 --> 00:16:58,330
Another thing we're going to look at

254
00:16:58,330 --> 00:17:00,170
was the presence of villus edema,

255
00:17:00,290 --> 00:17:01,990
the extent of villus edema, correct?

256
00:17:02,290 --> 00:17:04,010
And you'll recall that you could either have

257
00:17:04,010 --> 00:17:04,970
all of the moles,

258
00:17:05,350 --> 00:17:07,530
sorry, all of the villi be edeminous

259
00:17:07,530 --> 00:17:09,290
or some of the villi be edeminous.

260
00:17:09,730 --> 00:17:10,990
And in a complete mole,

261
00:17:11,250 --> 00:17:13,130
all of the villi are edeminous,

262
00:17:13,230 --> 00:17:15,510
i.e. they're completely edeminous, okay?

263
00:17:15,710 --> 00:17:16,690
And in a partial mole,

264
00:17:16,890 --> 00:17:17,950
some of the villi are edeminous,

265
00:17:17,990 --> 00:17:18,950
but we'll get there in a minute.

266
00:17:19,810 --> 00:17:21,670
And then we were also going to ask the question

267
00:17:21,670 --> 00:17:22,990
about trophoblastic proliferation.

268
00:17:22,990 --> 00:17:25,250
Remember I said that in a molar pregnancy,

269
00:17:25,390 --> 00:17:27,630
one of the key things is you have too many trophoblasts,

270
00:17:27,670 --> 00:17:29,410
which are the cells that normally align the villus.

271
00:17:29,750 --> 00:17:31,250
Well, it's possible that you could have

272
00:17:31,250 --> 00:17:33,410
the excess trophoblast present

273
00:17:33,410 --> 00:17:35,150
only along one side of the villus,

274
00:17:35,410 --> 00:17:37,630
or the excess trophoblasts could be present

275
00:17:37,630 --> 00:17:39,390
all along the villus,

276
00:17:39,470 --> 00:17:41,210
all along the circumference of the villus.

277
00:17:41,570 --> 00:17:42,670
And in a complete mole,

278
00:17:42,810 --> 00:17:45,290
you get complete proliferation of the trophoblast,

279
00:17:45,630 --> 00:17:46,990
and so you're going to have proliferation

280
00:17:46,990 --> 00:17:48,990
of the trophoblasts all along the villus,

281
00:17:49,290 --> 00:17:50,290
whereas in a partial mole,

282
00:17:50,290 --> 00:17:53,390
it'll just be partially along the edematous villi.

283
00:17:53,950 --> 00:17:55,710
And then another point we could raise is,

284
00:17:55,910 --> 00:17:57,130
and I haven't put it in your chart here,

285
00:17:57,210 --> 00:17:58,150
but just to help you remember,

286
00:17:58,510 --> 00:18:00,690
one of the questions you can ask is about the beta-HCG.

287
00:18:00,990 --> 00:18:02,190
Remember that in a complete mole,

288
00:18:02,270 --> 00:18:03,750
the beta-HCG is much higher

289
00:18:03,750 --> 00:18:05,090
than it would be in a partial mole.

290
00:18:05,450 --> 00:18:06,290
And in a complete mole,

291
00:18:06,350 --> 00:18:08,210
the reason why the beta-HCG is more higher

292
00:18:08,210 --> 00:18:10,550
is because you have more trophoblastic proliferation,

293
00:18:11,070 --> 00:18:13,350
and it's the syncytio trophoblasts

294
00:18:13,350 --> 00:18:14,830
that actually make the beta-HCG.

295
00:18:15,050 --> 00:18:16,950
So that becomes easy to remember as well.

296
00:18:17,390 --> 00:18:18,030
And then finally,

297
00:18:18,030 --> 00:18:20,030
the feared complication of a molar pregnancy

298
00:18:20,030 --> 00:18:21,250
is chorio-carcinoma.

299
00:18:21,750 --> 00:18:22,790
And we could ask the question,

300
00:18:22,930 --> 00:18:25,410
which mole has a greater risk for chorio-carcinoma?

301
00:18:25,790 --> 00:18:28,430
Well, the answer is that the complete mole

302
00:18:28,430 --> 00:18:30,990
has the complete risk for chorio-carcinoma,

303
00:18:31,150 --> 00:18:32,510
so it has the higher risk.

304
00:18:32,750 --> 00:18:34,930
And the risk is about 2% to 3%.

305
00:18:34,930 --> 00:18:37,210
So that's the notion of a complete mole.

306
00:18:37,470 --> 00:18:40,270
Now let's read together from the chart in your book.

307
00:18:40,330 --> 00:18:41,090
So what do I say here?

308
00:18:41,390 --> 00:18:44,910
A complete mole is an empty egg fertilized by two sperm,

309
00:18:45,310 --> 00:18:46,690
giving you 46 chromosomes.

310
00:18:46,910 --> 00:18:48,190
Fetal tissue is absent.

311
00:18:48,190 --> 00:18:49,230
It's completely molar.

312
00:18:49,870 --> 00:18:51,430
There is villus edema,

313
00:18:51,750 --> 00:18:54,790
and most villi, almost all villi, are hydropic.

314
00:18:54,850 --> 00:18:56,530
Hydropic means that they're full of water.

315
00:18:56,670 --> 00:18:57,350
They're edematous.

316
00:18:57,990 --> 00:19:00,670
The trophoblastic proliferation is diffuse.

317
00:19:00,670 --> 00:19:07,410
It completely involves the circumference of the villi.

318
00:19:08,050 --> 00:19:11,130
And the risk for chorio-carcinoma is high.

319
00:19:11,230 --> 00:19:13,370
It's 2% to 3%, relatively high.

320
00:19:13,870 --> 00:19:14,990
Now let's look at partial mole.

321
00:19:15,110 --> 00:19:18,230
And partial mole, the problem is that you've got a normal egg

322
00:19:18,230 --> 00:19:20,530
with the chromosomal material of mom,

323
00:19:20,790 --> 00:19:24,290
and two sperm come in and fertilize that normal egg.

324
00:19:24,290 --> 00:19:26,710
So when two sperm come in and fertilize that normal egg,

325
00:19:26,770 --> 00:19:28,970
we're now going to have 69 chromosomes, right?

326
00:19:29,070 --> 00:19:33,830
46 from dad and 23 from mom.

327
00:19:34,270 --> 00:19:35,810
Now fetal tissue, it's a partial mole,

328
00:19:35,910 --> 00:19:37,990
so it'll have part fetus, part mole.

329
00:19:38,310 --> 00:19:39,750
So fetal tissue will be present.

330
00:19:40,110 --> 00:19:40,930
It's a partial mole,

331
00:19:40,930 --> 00:19:43,650
so the villi will partially be edematous.

332
00:19:44,070 --> 00:19:47,430
And there will be partial trophoblastic proliferation

333
00:19:47,430 --> 00:19:49,330
around the circumference of those villi.

334
00:19:49,650 --> 00:19:53,010
And there is a partial risk for chorio-carcinoma,

335
00:19:53,150 --> 00:19:56,850
i.e. there is a relatively lower risk for chorio-carcinoma.

336
00:19:57,430 --> 00:20:00,290
This is what the villi look like in a molar pregnancy.

337
00:20:00,610 --> 00:20:03,610
You get these massively dilated villi,

338
00:20:04,070 --> 00:20:06,450
and you get this trophoblastic proliferation

339
00:20:06,450 --> 00:20:08,530
where there are too many trophoblasts.

340
00:20:08,530 --> 00:20:10,730
Now the treatment of a molar pregnancy

341
00:20:10,730 --> 00:20:12,430
is dilatation and curatage.

342
00:20:12,830 --> 00:20:14,910
So basically what happens is you dilate the cervix,

343
00:20:14,970 --> 00:20:15,630
and then you go in there,

344
00:20:15,730 --> 00:20:17,810
and you curate out that molar pregnancy.

345
00:20:18,310 --> 00:20:19,330
When that occurs,

346
00:20:19,470 --> 00:20:21,710
the next thing you can do is measure the beta-HCG.

347
00:20:22,030 --> 00:20:23,350
And the beta-HCG is helpful

348
00:20:23,350 --> 00:20:25,770
because it helps to tell you that you removed the mole.

349
00:20:26,170 --> 00:20:27,990
So once you do the DNC,

350
00:20:28,050 --> 00:20:29,670
and you pull away the molar tissue,

351
00:20:30,050 --> 00:20:31,350
you've now gotten rid of the mole,

352
00:20:31,450 --> 00:20:33,090
and so therefore as you've gotten rid of the mole,

353
00:20:33,170 --> 00:20:35,090
the beta-HCG should also go down.

354
00:20:35,610 --> 00:20:37,170
So that's the way by which you ensure

355
00:20:37,170 --> 00:20:40,550
that there's adequate removal of the molar tissue.

356
00:20:41,270 --> 00:20:43,050
The other use for the beta-HCG

357
00:20:43,050 --> 00:20:45,550
is to screen for the development of choriocarcinoma.

358
00:20:46,010 --> 00:20:47,550
Now remember that the feared complication

359
00:20:47,550 --> 00:20:49,690
of a molar pregnancy is choriocarcinoma.

360
00:20:50,110 --> 00:20:51,510
If a woman has a molar pregnancy,

361
00:20:51,750 --> 00:20:53,430
it's emotionally very distressing,

362
00:20:53,990 --> 00:20:55,150
and it's inconvenient.

363
00:20:55,850 --> 00:20:58,750
However, it's not potentially lethal.

364
00:20:59,170 --> 00:21:00,270
What's potentially lethal

365
00:21:00,270 --> 00:21:02,290
would be the development of a choriocarcinoma.

366
00:21:02,290 --> 00:21:04,330
That's the feared complication

367
00:21:04,330 --> 00:21:06,870
because that's a high-grade malignant tumor.

368
00:21:07,450 --> 00:21:09,770
And so that's what we want to screen for,

369
00:21:09,990 --> 00:21:12,310
and we can use the beta-HCG to screen for it.

370
00:21:12,370 --> 00:21:14,390
We can ask the patient to come in

371
00:21:14,390 --> 00:21:16,490
to get subsequent beta-HCG testing

372
00:21:16,490 --> 00:21:18,190
over the next year, for example,

373
00:21:18,550 --> 00:21:20,430
because it's usually within the first year

374
00:21:20,430 --> 00:21:23,270
after the removal of the molar pregnancy

375
00:21:23,270 --> 00:21:25,630
that a choriocarcinoma could classically develop.

376
00:21:26,070 --> 00:21:28,050
So the beta-HCG is useful for screening

377
00:21:28,850 --> 00:21:31,050
for the future development of choriocarcinoma

378
00:21:31,050 --> 00:21:32,070
in these patients as well.

379
00:21:32,830 --> 00:21:33,930
Now choriocarcinoma,

380
00:21:34,110 --> 00:21:35,750
just very briefly touching on this,

381
00:21:35,750 --> 00:21:37,550
remember that choriocarcinoma is what?

382
00:21:37,670 --> 00:21:39,030
It's a tumor of trophoblasts.

383
00:21:40,030 --> 00:21:41,030
So in a choriocarcinoma,

384
00:21:41,150 --> 00:21:44,030
you have cytotrophoblasts and syncytiotrophoblasts.

385
00:21:44,270 --> 00:21:46,470
The syncytiotrophoblasts produce beta-HCG,

386
00:21:46,830 --> 00:21:49,390
and remember that there will be no villi.

387
00:21:49,730 --> 00:21:50,570
You won't see villi.

388
00:21:50,630 --> 00:21:53,210
You will only see the cytotrophoblasts

389
00:21:53,210 --> 00:21:54,830
and the syncytiotrophoblasts,

390
00:21:54,830 --> 00:21:56,670
and that's what defines choriocarcinoma.

391
00:21:57,090 --> 00:21:59,330
Now, it's important to note

392
00:21:59,330 --> 00:22:01,510
that if you put together the discussions

393
00:22:01,510 --> 00:22:02,710
that we've had in this chapter,

394
00:22:05,750 --> 00:22:08,830
remember that one of the subtypes of germ cell tumor

395
00:22:08,830 --> 00:22:10,070
was a choriocarcinoma.

396
00:22:10,530 --> 00:22:11,950
Now, that choriocarcinoma,

397
00:22:12,030 --> 00:22:13,830
that has nothing to do with gestation.

398
00:22:14,530 --> 00:22:16,830
It did not arise in a background of,

399
00:22:17,090 --> 00:22:19,910
for example, a mole and a patient being pregnant

400
00:22:19,910 --> 00:22:21,250
or thinking that they were pregnant.

401
00:22:21,730 --> 00:22:24,170
So that is one way to get a choriocarcinoma,

402
00:22:24,270 --> 00:22:25,650
which is as a germ cell tumor,

403
00:22:25,750 --> 00:22:27,430
a spontaneous germ cell tumor.

404
00:22:28,090 --> 00:22:29,930
Another way to get a choriocarcinoma

405
00:22:29,930 --> 00:22:32,030
would be as a complication of gestation.

406
00:22:32,030 --> 00:22:35,810
So, for example, about half of choriocarcinoma

407
00:22:35,810 --> 00:22:38,230
arises as a complication of a molar pregnancy.

408
00:22:38,790 --> 00:22:40,630
Sometimes choriocarcinoma can arise

409
00:22:40,630 --> 00:22:42,850
as a complication of a spontaneous abortion.

410
00:22:43,510 --> 00:22:45,270
Sometimes choriocarcinoma can arise

411
00:22:45,270 --> 00:22:47,810
as a complication even after a normal pregnancy.

412
00:22:48,770 --> 00:22:50,950
Of course, choriocarcinoma is exceedingly rare,

413
00:22:51,090 --> 00:22:51,830
very, very rare,

414
00:22:52,210 --> 00:22:54,470
but this is still another way

415
00:22:54,470 --> 00:22:55,510
by which that can occur.

416
00:22:56,070 --> 00:22:58,850
So, you actually have two separate pathways

417
00:22:58,850 --> 00:23:00,110
for choriocarcinoma.

418
00:23:00,110 --> 00:23:02,230
Now, how do examiners go after this?

419
00:23:02,570 --> 00:23:03,870
The way they test this is

420
00:23:03,870 --> 00:23:06,790
the fact that the gestational pathway,

421
00:23:06,910 --> 00:23:08,770
the pathway, for example, after a mole,

422
00:23:09,230 --> 00:23:10,730
responds well to chemotherapy.

423
00:23:11,310 --> 00:23:12,310
But remember I told you

424
00:23:12,310 --> 00:23:14,630
that the germ cell pathway does not.

425
00:23:15,370 --> 00:23:17,690
So this is the way by which examiners

426
00:23:17,690 --> 00:23:18,510
will go after this

427
00:23:18,510 --> 00:23:21,330
because it ensures to the examiners

428
00:23:21,330 --> 00:23:22,570
that you actually understand

429
00:23:22,570 --> 00:23:25,250
that choriocarcinoma is actually kind of two flavors.

430
00:23:26,010 --> 00:23:28,870
So when choriocarcinoma arises as a complication,

431
00:23:28,870 --> 00:23:32,370
just a normal spontaneous germ cell tumor,

432
00:23:33,010 --> 00:23:35,230
then that will not respond well

433
00:23:35,230 --> 00:23:36,630
to chemotherapy classically.

434
00:23:37,070 --> 00:23:39,030
Whereas when choriocarcinoma arises

435
00:23:39,030 --> 00:23:41,370
as a complication of gestation,

436
00:23:41,570 --> 00:23:43,750
for example, after a molar pregnancy,

437
00:23:44,430 --> 00:23:47,090
then that will respond very well to chemotherapy.

438
00:23:47,330 --> 00:23:48,990
And it's amazing how these tumors

439
00:23:48,990 --> 00:23:49,950
can respond to chemotherapy.

440
00:23:50,130 --> 00:23:52,370
They literally can just melt and disappear.

441
00:23:52,370 --> 00:23:55,370
So that's a very important distinction

442
00:23:55,370 --> 00:23:57,290
both clinically, it's a practical principle,

443
00:23:57,430 --> 00:23:58,990
it's a high-yield quote-unquote

444
00:23:58,990 --> 00:24:02,510
high-yield topic to be aware of

445
00:24:02,510 --> 00:24:04,330
or high-yield principle to be aware of.

446
00:24:04,950 --> 00:24:06,770
So that concludes our discussion

447
00:24:06,770 --> 00:24:09,290
of choriocarcinoma and gestational topics,

448
00:24:09,470 --> 00:24:11,370
and that concludes our chapter as well.

449
00:24:11,370 --> 00:24:13,710
Let's do it.

